% Integration example: air-passenger compensation under EU Regulation 261/2004
% (LE_extensions_proposal §5), exercising the constructs together:
% provenance trailers and "scenario facts require provenance" (§17.1), judged
% templates decided by precedent through an "otherwise" cascade and the
% precedent library (§17.2, §17.8), a decision table for the remedy (§17.3),
% the applicability / question / remedy sections (§17.4), a source-scoped
% proof — extraordinary circumstances must be established by the CARRIER's
% evidence (§17.5) — and a flip query (§17.7).
%
% The facts are those of the CJEU's Wallentin-Hermann judgment (C-549/07): a
% Vienna-Brindisi flight cancelled because of a turbine defect found in a check
% the day before; the Court held that technical problems which come to light
% during maintenance are inherent in the normal activity of a carrier, and so
% not extraordinary circumstances. In the new claim, Alitalia's own evidence
% shows a maintenance problem: by analogy to Wallentin-Hermann it is inherent,
% the carrier cannot establish extraordinary circumstances, and the passenger
% is owed the band-a amount for an 800 km flight. A bird strike, by contrast,
% is forced the other way by Peskova (C-315/15): with the carrier's evidence
% that it was beyond its control, the carrier establishes extraordinary
% circumstances and owes nothing.

the target language is: prolog.
scenario facts require provenance.

the knowledge base eu261 includes these resources:
    precedent.

the templates are:
    *a passenger* is entitled to compensation of *an amount* for *a flight*.
    *a passenger* is booked on *a flight*; undefined.
    *a flight* is within scope.
    *a flight* departs from *an airport*; undefined.
    *an airport* is in the EU.
    *a flight* is cancelled; undefined.
    *a passenger* is notified of the cancellation of *a flight* with *a number* days of notice; undefined.
    the cancellation of *a flight* is caused by *an event*; undefined.
    the cancellation of *a flight* is due to extraordinary circumstances.
    *an event* is inherent in the normal exercise of the activity of *a carrier*; judged.
    *an event* counts as inherent in the normal exercise of the activity of *a carrier*.
    *an event* is beyond the actual control of *a carrier*; judged.
    the carrier of *a flight* is *a carrier*; undefined.
    the distance of *a flight* is *a number* km; undefined.
    *an amount* is the compensation due for *a flight*.
    the compensation for a distance of *a number* km is *an amount* under table article_7.
    *an event* is a technical problem discovered during maintenance of the aircraft of *a carrier*; undefined.
    *an event* is a hidden manufacturing defect revealed by the manufacturer or an authority; undefined.
    *an event* is a collision with a bird; undefined.

the table article_7 is, with first match:
    band | distance km            | amount
    a    | <= 1500                | 250
    b    | > 1500 and <= 3500     | 400
    c    | > 3500                 | 600

the knowledge base eu261 includes:

section applicability is:

Vienna is in the EU.

a flight is within scope
    if the flight departs from an airport
    and the airport is in the EU.

section question is:

a passenger is entitled to compensation of an amount for a flight
    if the passenger is booked on the flight
    and the flight is within scope
    and the flight is cancelled
    and it is not the case that
        the passenger is notified of the cancellation of the flight with a number days of notice
        and the number >= 14
    and the carrier of the flight is a carrier
    and it is not the case that
        the cancellation of the flight is due to extraordinary circumstances
        according to the carrier
    and the amount is the compensation due for the flight.

the cancellation of a flight is due to extraordinary circumstances
    if the cancellation of the flight is caused by an event
    and the carrier of the flight is a carrier
    and it is not the case that
        the event counts as inherent in the normal exercise of the activity of the carrier
    and the event is beyond the actual control of the carrier.

% The judged predicate, decided by precedent when a precedent forces it.
an event counts as inherent in the normal exercise of the activity of a carrier
    if the event is forced for inherent by a case
    otherwise the event is inherent in the normal exercise of the activity of the carrier
    and it is not the case that
        the event is forced against inherent by a case.

an event has factor maintenance problem
    if the event is a technical problem discovered during maintenance of the aircraft of a carrier.
an event has factor hidden manufacturing defect
    if the event is a hidden manufacturing defect revealed by the manufacturer or an authority.
an event has factor bird strike
    if the event is a collision with a bird.

maintenance problem favours inherent.
hidden manufacturing defect disfavours inherent.
bird strike disfavours inherent.

wallentin hermann has factor maintenance problem, as stated in C-549/07 at paragraph 11.
wallentin hermann decided for inherent,
    according to the CJEU, as stated in C-549/07 at paragraph 25,
    because "technical problems which come to light during maintenance cannot constitute, in themselves, extraordinary circumstances".
peskova has factor bird strike, as stated in C-315/15 at paragraph 24.
peskova decided against inherent,
    according to the CJEU, as stated in C-315/15 at paragraph 26,
    because "a collision with a bird is not intrinsically linked to the operating system of the aircraft".

section remedy is:

an amount is the compensation due for a flight
    if the distance of the flight is a number km
    and the compensation for a distance of the number km is the amount under table article_7.

scenario new_claim is:
    anna is booked on flight AZ123, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 departs from Vienna, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 is cancelled, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 2.
    the carrier of flight AZ123 is Alitalia, according to Alitalia, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 1.
    the cancellation of flight AZ123 is caused by the inspection defect,
        according to Alitalia, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 3.
    the inspection defect is a technical problem discovered during maintenance of the aircraft of Alitalia,
        according to Alitalia, as stated in the maintenance log at entry 14.
    the distance of flight AZ123 is 800 km, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    claim expects answers ["anna is entitled to compensation of 250 for flight AZ123"].
    stage expects answers [].
    consistency expects answers [].
    flip expects changes [["remove: anna is booked on flight AZ123"], ["remove: flight AZ123 departs from Vienna"], ["remove: flight AZ123 is cancelled"], ["remove: the carrier of flight AZ123 is Alitalia"], ["remove: the distance of flight AZ123 is 800 km"]].

scenario notified is:
    anna is booked on flight AZ123, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 departs from Vienna, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 is cancelled, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 2.
    anna is notified of the cancellation of flight AZ123 with 20 days of notice,
        according to Alitalia, as stated in the notice email.
    the carrier of flight AZ123 is Alitalia, according to Alitalia, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 1.
    the distance of flight AZ123 is 800 km, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    claim expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

scenario bird_strike is:
    anna is booked on flight AZ123, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 departs from Vienna, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight AZ123 is cancelled, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 2.
    the carrier of flight AZ123 is Alitalia, according to Alitalia, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 1.
    the cancellation of flight AZ123 is caused by the bird collision,
        according to Alitalia, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 3.
    the bird collision is a collision with a bird, according to Alitalia, as stated in the incident report at page 1.
    the bird collision is beyond the actual control of Alitalia,
        according to Alitalia, as stated in the incident report at page 2,
        because "the bird struck the engine on take-off".
    the distance of flight AZ123 is 800 km, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    claim expects answers [].

scenario outside_the_eu is:
    anna is booked on flight XY9, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight XY9 departs from Istanbul, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    flight XY9 is cancelled, as stated in the carrier letter at paragraph 2.
    the carrier of flight XY9 is Skyline, according to Skyline.
    the distance of flight XY9 is 1200 km, as stated in the booking at page 1.
    claim expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at applicability"].

query claim is:
    which passenger is entitled to compensation of which amount for which flight.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query consistency is:
    which issue has an inconsistent case base.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        it is not the case that anna is entitled to compensation of 250 for flight AZ123.
